
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third question — module format. This one's also hard to reverse, since it determines how every form file loads the
  validator.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the repo is split today. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m loads as a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m (no modules, no build), while [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m uses
  CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m. A shared validator has to pick one, and that choice propagates to every form page and to how tests
  import it.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m, loaded via [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m in the browser and natively importable in Node (needs
    [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m or [38;5;153m.mjs[39m). Buys one format for both browser and tests, no build step, and it's where
    the platform has landed. Costs: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m needs [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m's [38;5;153mrequire[39m would either stay as-is
    (mixed repo) or get converted.
  - [1mCommonJS + global fallback[0m — module exports for tests, plus a [38;5;153mwindow.FormValidation[39m assignment for the browser. Buys
    consistency with the existing [38;5;153msrc/[39m files and no HTML change. Costs: the dual-export dance is boilerplate in every
    file, and it's the format you'd migrate away from later anyway.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — esbuild/Vite, write whatever, ship a bundle. Buys real dependency management if this grows. Costs: a
    build step, a config, and a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m for a project that currently has zero dependencies. Clearly premature
    here.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m No build, works natively in both places, and the only migration cost is one
  [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m attribute. I'd leave [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m's CommonJS alone — it's unrelated to this work, and converting it is
  the kind of drive-by refactor that doesn't serve the goal.
